[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .   # -> c_src/build/driver
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd translation && cargo build --release                                 # -> translation/target/release/driver
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd translation && cargo test                                            # differential suite
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 1. Exit status diverged when stdout is a pipe with a closed read end
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ echo 1 | ./c_src/build/driver           > >(exec 0<&-; :) ; echo $?
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 141
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ echo 1 | ./translation/target/release/driver > >(exec 0<&-; :) ; echo $?
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout and stderr were both empty and identical; only the exit status differed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `141` is `128 + 13`, i.e. killed by `SIGPIPE`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` during runtime setup,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before `main` is entered. A write to a broken pipe therefore returns `EPIPE`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m instead of raising a signal, and because `print_line` discards its write result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the process went on to exit 0. The C program keeps the default disposition and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is killed by signal 13 — for `x != 0` at the `printf` in `printLine`, and for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `x == 0` at the stdout flush that glibc performs while exiting.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `reset_sigpipe()` in `src/main.rs` calls `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)` as the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m statement of `main`, restoring C's disposition. `libc` is already linked on the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `*-unknown-linux-gnu` targets, so the symbol is declared in an `extern "C"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m block rather than pulling in a dependency.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Regression test**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `closed_stdout_pipe_kills_both_the_same_way`. The child is spawned with all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m three streams piped and the read end of its stdout is dropped *while the child
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is still blocked in `scanf`*, before stdin has been written. The failure is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m therefore deterministic rather than a race.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 2. Unconsumed stdin was not handed back, so a later reader of the same descriptor saw different bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ printf '1 REST-OF-FILE\nsecond line\n' > f
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ { ./c_src/build/driver; cat; } < f
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m string
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m  REST-OF-FILE
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m second line
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ { ./translation/target/release/driver; cat; } < f
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m string
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m With a 20001-byte file, `{ driver >/dev/null; wc -c; } < f` reported 20000 bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m left for C and 11809 (= 20001 − 8192) for Rust.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The translation read stdin through `std::io::Stdin`, which wraps the descriptor
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in an 8 KiB `BufReader`. Two separate errors followed from that:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **No offset restoration.** glibc, while tearing the stream down at exit, seeks
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   a seekable descriptor back by the part of the `FILE` buffer that was never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   consumed, leaving the offset exactly past the last byte the conversion used.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Rust's `BufReader` just drops its buffer, permanently losing those bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Wrong buffer size.** glibc sizes the buffer from `st_blksize`, which is 4096
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   for both regular files and pipes on Linux; Rust's `BufReader` uses 8192. On a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   *pipe* nothing can be seeked back, so the buffer size alone decides how much a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   single `scanf` removes from the stream. For a 60001-byte pipe the leftover was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   55905 bytes (60001 − 4096) for C and 51809 (60001 − 8192) for Rust.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `CStdin` in `src/main.rs` was rewritten to model glibc's `FILE` directly: a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4096-byte buffer filled by `read(2)` on fd 0, a read cursor, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `restore_offset()`, which `main` calls at exit to `lseek(0, -(unconsumed),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m SEEK_CUR)`. The `lseek` fails with `ESPIPE` on a pipe and the failure is ignored,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m which is also the net effect in glibc.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `ungetc` is now modelled by stepping the read cursor back one byte instead of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m holding the byte in a side channel, so a pushed-back byte counts as unconsumed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and is included in the offset that gets handed back. This matters: for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `aREST-OF-FILE` the C program leaves the `a` readable, and for `+aREST-OF-FILE`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m it leaves `aREST-OF-FILE` — the sign is consumed, only the single byte that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stopped the conversion is pushed back.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
